{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","count":3,"questions":[{"id":52169,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A specially designed Vernier calliper has the main scale least count of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math>. On the Vernier scale, there are 10 equal divisions and they match with 11 main scale divisions. Then, the least count of the Vernier calliper is","options":[" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math><br />\n ","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>909</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math><br />\n ","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math><br />\n ","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>09</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>Here, 10 divisions of vernier scall<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></math> main scale divisions So, 1 vernier scale division <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>11</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac></math> main scale divisions</p>\n\n<p>Now, we use formula for least count, Least count <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> main scale division -1 vernier scale division.</p>\n\n<p><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⇒</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>L</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>V</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>D</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>11</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>D</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>D</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>m</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>01</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>m</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>So, magnitude of least count is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math>.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SA)","_marksId":"614da48bf647084f67c0d955"},{"id":52170,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In an experiment on simple pendulum to determine the acceleration due to gravity, a student measures the elngth of the thread as $632 \\mathrm{~cm}$ and diameter of the pendulum bob as $2.256 \\mathrm{~cm}$. The student should take the lenght of the pendulum to be","options":["$64.328 \\mathrm{~cm}$","$64.36 \\mathrm{~cm}$","$65.456$","$65.5 \\mathrm{~cm}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"length should be taken up to com\n$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\ell_{\\text {net }}=63.2+\\frac{2.256}{2} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow 64.328\n\\end{array}\nby signlicont figures\n$\\ell_{\\text {net }} \\Rightarrow 64.3 \\mathrm{~cm}","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SA)","_marksId":"6174d21325e10c3f7d508752"},{"id":52171,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The figure of a centimeter scale below shows a particular position of the vernier calipers. In this position the value of $x$ shown in the figure is ( figure is not to scale)\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fo0vXpeiEe5bBOLlqhxYUeC85I4shc9iZvXveHCbblTs.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fo0vXpeiEe5bBOLlqhxYUeC85I4shc9iZvXveHCbblTs.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fo0vXpeiEe5bBOLlqhxYUeC85I4shc9iZvXveHCbblTs.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$0.02 \\mathrm{~cm}$","$3.65 \\mathrm{~cm}$","$4.15 \\mathrm{~cm}$","$0.03 \\mathrm{~cm}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\mathrm{x}=0.3 \\mathrm{~cm}-3 \\times$ scale division of vernier calipers\n\n\\begin{array}{l}\n=0.3 \\mathrm{~cm}-3 \\times \\frac{9}{100} \\\\\n=\\frac{30-27}{100} \\\\\n=\\frac{3}{100} \\\\\n=0.03 \\mathrm{~cm}\n\\end{array}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21317806d30fe0d0021"}]}